    "body": "#34959 hardened `BerkeleyRODatabase::Open()` against circular references by\r\nvalidating btree page levels and bounding overflow reads by the stated data\r\nlength. Both checks work, but they only stop a page from being reached again\r\n*through itself*. A page reached again through a different path is still parsed\r\nagain, and there are two ways to do that which the current checks do not catch:\r\n\r\n- A btree page can be referenced by more than one parent. It stays at a level\r\n  one below each of them, so the level check passes, and the page and its whole\r\n  subtree are parsed once for every path that reaches it. With a page holding\r\n  ~34 duplicate child references per level, the work grows by 34x per level\r\n  while the file only grows by about a page. A depth-7 file of 8 KB does not\r\n  finish.\r\n\r\n- An overflow page with no data (`hf_offset == 0`) never advances the\r\n  accumulated length, so the `data.size() > item_len` guard never fires. Two\r\n  such pages pointing at each other loop forever.\r\n\r\nBoth are reachable through `migratewallet` (and any other path that opens a\r\nlegacy file with the read-only parser) and hang the process on current master\r\nat 100% CPU. Neither corrupts memory; this is CPU/memory exhaustion from a\r\ncrafted or corrupted wallet file, the same threat model #34959 was addressing.\r\n\r\nThis is the visited-page approach from #34946 / #35150, which were closed in\r\nfavour of #34959 on the understanding that the level and length checks were a\r\nsuperset of them. For the two cases above they are not, so the visited-page\r\nsets are added back: one for the btree DFS, one per overflow chain, throwing on\r\nthe first page that is seen twice. A valid BDB database references every page\r\nexactly once, so this rejects nothing that parses today.",
